        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mycenaean civilisation</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from c. 1450 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and outposts such as Miletus on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> (Greece and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> today)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1600–1100 BCE; the shaft graves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> c. 1600–1500 BCE, the palace states c. 1400–1200 BCE, then destruction around 1200 BCE and a long decline</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The citadels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiryns">Tiryns</a> with their Cyclopean walls and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a>; the gold of the shaft graves; tholos tombs such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treasury-of-atreus">Treasury of Atreus</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets, the oldest written Greek; the heroes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s epics</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated citadels, palaces and tombs (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> from 1876, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-blegen">Carl Blegen</a> at Pylos from 1939); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets, deciphered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ventris">Michael Ventris</a> in 1952; Hittite letters about Ahhiyawa and Egyptian lists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> places; later Greek epic and myth, which preserve memories rather than history</span></div>
        </div>
