    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>From about 1600 BCE warrior chiefs on the Greek mainland grew rich, as the gold of the shaft graves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> shows, and by 1400 BCE kings ruled palace states at Mycenae, Pylos, Thebes and elsewhere.</li><li>Their scribes wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, deciphered in 1952 as the earliest known form of Greek, to record land, livestock, workers, weapons and offerings to gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>.</li><li>Around 1200 BCE the palaces were destroyed and writing was forgotten; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s epics, composed centuries later, remember <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>’s city, but they are evidence of memory, not of fact.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> are all named on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets of about 1200 BCE from Pylos, the oldest written records of the Greek gods, some five centuries before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

