    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1600 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Rich burials in the shaft graves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> mark the rise of a warrior elite</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1450 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> take over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> comes into use</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1250 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> are extended and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a> is built</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">13th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Hittite king writes to the king of Ahhiyawa as his “brother” in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawagalawa-letter">Tawagalawa letter</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A wave of destruction ends the palaces; Pylos burns around 1180 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1100 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The last Mycenaean communities fade as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> enters the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1876</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> excavates the shaft graves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a">Grave Circle A</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1952</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ventris">Michael Ventris</a> deciphers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, showing that it records Greek</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a.jpg" alt="Grave Circle A" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a">Grave Circle A</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece-0388_(2215117229).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mycenaean civilisation takes its modern name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>, the citadel in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> that Greek legend made the seat of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a>. Its people spoke Greek, as their writing shows, but what they called themselves is uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> calls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> who fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> Achaeans, Danaans and Argives, and Hittite texts speak of a land called Ahhiyawa, a name most scholars connect with the Achaeans.</p><p>In the Early Helladic period of the 3rd millennium BCE mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> had prosperous settlements, some with large buildings such as the House of the Tiles at Lerna, but many were destroyed or abandoned towards its end. The Middle Helladic period that followed, from about 2000 BCE, was poorer: small villages, simple graves and a grey wheel-made pottery called Minyan ware. When speakers of Greek, or of the language from which it grew, arrived in Greece is debated; many scholars place it around the start of this period, others earlier or later. A study of ancient DNA published in 2017 found the Mycenaeans genetically close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, but with a share of ancestry related to peoples of the Eurasian steppe or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> that the Minoans lacked, which fits, though it does not prove, an arrival of Indo-European speakers from the north or east.</p><p>Change came fast in the 17th and 16th centuries BCE. At Mycenae two circles of deep shaft graves, Grave Circle B, begun before 1600 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a">Grave Circle A</a>, of about 1600–1500 BCE, held men, women and children buried with gold masks, diadems and cups, bronze swords, daggers inlaid with hunting scenes in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, Baltic amber and objects in Cretan style. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> found Grave Circle A in 1876; Ioannis Papadimitriou and George Mylonas excavated Grave Circle B from 1952. These burials reveal a warrior elite enriched by war, raiding and trade. In Messenia, in the south-west, families of the same rank built the earliest tholos tombs, stone chambers roofed with a beehive-shaped dome.</p><p>By the 15th century BCE mainland rulers were adopting Cretan art, crafts and methods of administration. Around 1450 BCE Mycenaean Greeks took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> on Crete, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, adapted from the Minoan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> to write Greek, appears in quantity for the first time.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-nestor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palace-of-nestor.jpg" alt="Palace of Nestor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-nestor">Palace of Nestor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20190508_088_pylos_nestor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean Housen, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the 14th and 13th centuries BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was divided among palace states centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiryns">Tiryns</a>, Midea, Pylos, Thebes, Orchomenos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, among others, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. Each seems to have been ruled by a king, the wanax, who headed the administration and had a part in religion. Next to him the tablets name the lawagetas, “leader of the people”, perhaps a commander, and below them landholders, officials and district governors. The qa-si-re-u, the word that became the later Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a>, “king”, was then a lesser local figure, in one case the head of a group of bronze-smiths.</p><p>The best-documented kingdom is Pylos in Messenia, whose palace, found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-blegen">Carl Blegen</a> in 1939, is called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-nestor">Palace of Nestor</a> after the old king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>. Its tablets describe a state divided into two provinces of nine and seven districts and record land holdings, flocks, levies in kind, bronze issued to smiths, and rations of grain and figs for dependent workers. Among these were women textile workers and their children, some identified by the names of places on the Anatolian coast such as Miletus and Knidos, perhaps captives or bought slaves. The palaces ran specialised industries, above all textiles, perfumed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and bronze working, but most farming and much exchange lay outside their records, and ordinary villagers appear in them only as names, numbers and obligations.</p><p>Society was organised for war. The tablets from Knossos list <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, wheels and armour, and tombs and settlements have yielded long bronze swords, spearheads, helmets covered with plates of boar’s tusk such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> later described, and a full suit of bronze armour from a tomb at Dendra in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a>, of about 1400 BCE. Rulers fortified their citadels with walls of huge, roughly fitted blocks, called Cyclopean because later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> believed that only the giant Cyclopes could have moved them. Mycenae, Tiryns, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a> and the vast fortress of Gla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> were walled in this way, and in the 13th century BCE Mycenae, Tiryns and Athens built protected passages down to water sources, as if expecting sieges.</p><p>Most people lived in villages, growing wheat, barley, olives and vines and keeping sheep and goats; the tablets of Knossos alone record tens of thousands of sheep, raised above all for wool.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/linear-b"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/linear-b.jpg" alt="Linear B" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Linear_B_tablet_of_Pylos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sharon Mollerus, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets are the oldest written evidence for Greek religion. They name many gods who were still worshipped a thousand years later: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, especially honoured at Pylos; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>; Enyalios, a war god whose name later became a title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>; Paiawon, later a title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>; and Eleuthia, goddess of childbirth. The most frequent title is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potnia">Potnia</a>, “Lady” or “Mistress”, given to one or more goddesses, as in the “Potnia of the labyrinth” at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and the “Potnia of the horses” at Pylos, and a “Potnia of Atana” at Knossos may be an early form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. Other deities of the tablets, such as a “Divine Mother” and Drimios, son of Zeus, later disappeared.</p><p>The tablets record offerings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, honey, wine, grain, livestock and perfumes, and goods delivered to shrines and to priests, priestesses and “slaves of the god”. One tablet from Pylos, Tn 316, lists gold vessels and men and women sent to several deities; whether the people were meant for sacrifice, as some scholars have argued, or for service in the shrines is disputed.</p><p>Archaeology shows the places of cult. Within the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> a group of shrines, the Cult Centre, contained clay figures of deities or worshippers, altars and wall paintings, and a shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phylakopi">Phylakopi</a> on Melos held similar figures. Small clay figurines shaped like the Greek letters phi and psi, perhaps protective images, are found in great numbers in houses, tombs and shrines.</p><p>The dead were usually buried, not cremated: families in chamber tombs cut into hillsides, rulers in tholos tombs, great stone domes covered with earth. Bodies were laid out with jewellery, weapons, vessels and food, and earlier burials were often pushed aside to make room for new ones. Centuries later, in the 8th century BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> left offerings in some Mycenaean tombs, honouring the unknown dead as heroes of a legendary past. Much of this religion passed into Ancient Greek religion, although its palace rituals ended with the palaces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mask-of-agamemnon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mask-of-agamemnon.jpg" alt="Mask of Agamemnon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mask-of-agamemnon">Mask of Agamemnon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gleb Simonov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mycenaean builders and artists borrowed much from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and made it their own. The heart of each palace was the megaron, a large hall entered through a porch and vestibule, with a round hearth between four columns. At Pylos the throne stood against one wall, flanked by painted lions and griffins, and another painting showed a lyre player. Wall paintings of processions of women, hunts and battles, in a style learned from Minoan artists, decorated the palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiryns">Tiryns</a>, Pylos, Thebes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>.</p><p>The greatest monuments are at Mycenae. Around 1250 BCE its walls were extended and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a> built, with a relief of two lions facing a column above the lintel. The Treasury of Atreus, a tholos tomb of about the same date, has a corbelled stone dome about 13 metres high, the largest in the world for more than a thousand years. Engineers drained much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-copais">Lake Copais</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> with dikes and canals to create farmland, and built roads with bridges of Cyclopean masonry, such as the bridge at Arkadiko on the road between Tiryns and Epidaurus, which still stands.</p><p>Craftsmen excelled in small works: the gold cups from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vapheio">Vapheio</a> of about 1500 BCE, with scenes of bulls being caught; bronze daggers inlaid with gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>; carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, such as a group of two women and a child from Mycenae; and engraved sealstones, among them a tiny agate found in 2015 in the grave of the “Griffin Warrior” near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-nestor">Palace of Nestor</a>, which shows a combat in astonishing detail. Potters made fine painted wares in great quantity, including stirrup jars for oil and wine. The Warrior Vase from Mycenae, of the 12th century BCE, shows soldiers marching out while a woman raises her arm, perhaps in farewell.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> was a syllabary of about ninety signs, used with further signs for commodities and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimal-system">decimal system</a> of numbers. Scribes wrote on clay tablets probably kept only for the current year; they survive because the fires that destroyed the palaces baked them hard. Tablets come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, Chania, Pylos, Mycenae, Tiryns, Thebes and other sites, and painted inscriptions appear on storage jars. They record administration only: no laws, letters or literature survive. Alice Kober showed in the 1940s that the language was inflected; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ventris">Michael Ventris</a>, an architect, announced in 1952 that it was an early form of Greek, and a tablet from Pylos with pictures of tripods and vessels, read in 1953, confirmed it. With John Chadwick he published Documents in Mycenaean Greek in 1956.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sea-peoples.jpg" alt="Sea Peoples" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist in pay of Ramesses III, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mycenaeans traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Their painted pottery, much of it containers for wine and perfumed oil, is found from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>’s city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a>. In return came <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from Cyprus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, glass and Baltic amber, and a hoard of Near Eastern cylinder seals of lapis lazuli was found in the palace of Thebes. The Uluburun shipwreck, which sank off south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in the late 14th century BCE and was excavated between 1984 and 1994, shows the scale of this trade: it carried about ten tonnes of copper and one of tin, with glass ingots, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, ivory, Canaanite jars and Mycenaean pottery.</p><p>The great powers knew of them. A list of foreign places on a statue base from the mortuary temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> at Thebes in Egypt, of the early 14th century BCE, names places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> on the mainland. More than twenty Hittite texts of about 1400–1200 BCE mention Ahhiyawa, which Emil Forrer identified with the Achaeans in 1924; most scholars now accept that it was a Mycenaean kingdom, though whether its king ruled from Mycenae, Thebes or elsewhere is debated. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawagalawa-letter">Tawagalawa letter</a>, probably written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a>, the Hittite king addresses the king of Ahhiyawa as his “brother”, complains of the raider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piyamaradu">Piyamaradu</a>, who operated from Millawanda (Miletus), and recalls an earlier quarrel over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilusa">Wilusa</a>, usually identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. In a treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv">Tudhaliya IV</a> the king of Ahhiyawa was written among the great kings of the age and then struck out. Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> swung between diplomacy and conflict on the Anatolian coast, where Mycenaeans lived at Miletus from about 1450 BCE and their pottery reached Troy.</p><p>Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, Mycenaean culture took the place of Minoan influence after 1450 BCE, spreading across Crete, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. At the end of the period, Egyptian inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> list among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> groups such as the Ekwesh and the Denyen, whom some scholars connect with the Achaeans and the Danaans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a>, who settled in the southern Levant in the 12th century BCE, at first made pottery derived from late Mycenaean styles, which suggests that some of them came from the Aegean world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lion-gate.jpg" alt="Lion Gate" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lion_Gate_-_Mycenae_by_Joy_of_Museums.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joyofmuseums, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From about 1250 BCE the palace world showed signs of strain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> suffered a first destruction. Around 1200 BCE a wave of destruction struck the palaces. Mycenae was damaged again around 1190 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiryns">Tiryns</a> and Thebes were destroyed, Gla was abandoned and the palace of Pylos burned around 1180 BCE. Tablets from its last months record “watchers guarding the coast” and the calling up of rowers, perhaps preparations against an attack by sea. Whether earthquakes, drought and famine, revolt, raiders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> or the breakdown of the trade on which the palaces depended were to blame is debated; many scholars think several combined. The old theory of a “Dorian invasion” from the north, based on Greek legends of the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>’ descendants, has little archaeological support. The collapse was part of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a> that also brought down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p><p>Life went on without the palaces. In the 12th century BCE the lower town of Tiryns grew, Mycenae survived on a smaller scale, and people moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, the islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, where Greek speakers settled; the Cypriot dialect, like the Arcadian, preserves features close to the language of the tablets. Writing was forgotten, and by about 1100 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> had entered the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a>, the names of the gods and many place names survived, but in Greece and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> writing returned only in the 8th century BCE, with an alphabet adapted from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>.</p><p>The Mycenaeans lived on in memory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>, composed probably in the 8th or 7th century BCE from a long oral tradition, remember Mycenae “rich in gold”, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor of Pylos</a>, and preserve old details such as the boar’s-tusk helmet. But the poems also reflect the world of their own time, and no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablet names Agamemnon or a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>: they are evidence of memory, not of fact. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> observed in the 5th century BCE that Mycenae had become a small place, and Pausanias, in the 2nd century CE, saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a> and graves said to be those of Atreus and Agamemnon.</p><p>Schliemann, guided by Pausanias, dug inside the walls of Mycenae in 1876, and Christos Tsountas, Alan Wace and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-blegen">Carl Blegen</a> made the study systematic. The decipherment of Linear B settled a long dispute between Evans, who saw the mainland as a dependency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, and Wace and Blegen, who argued for an independent mainland culture: Greek-speaking Mycenaeans had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> itself.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Mycenaean civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, from about 1600 to 1100 BCE, created the earliest known Greek-speaking states, ruled from palaces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> and Pylos and recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>. Its citadels, tombs and trade reached across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and after its collapse around 1200 BCE its memory, reshaped by centuries of storytelling, became the heroic age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s epics.</em></p>

